{
  "case_id": "2001-01-521",
  "document_hash": "52180c45b950d77af847337def6a1d14bddbf6876f6a15ff332175c479834fde",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2001-01-521",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "52180c45b950d77af847337def6a1d14bddbf6876f6a15ff332175c479834fde",
      "evidence_id": "ev-52180c45b950-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Could we find out from you just what the Announce Clause prohibits that isn't already prohibited by the Pledges and Promises Clause, as it's been interpreted?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "James Bopp, Jr. (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2001-01-521",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "52180c45b950d77af847337def6a1d14bddbf6876f6a15ff332175c479834fde",
      "evidence_id": "ev-52180c45b950-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BOPP: It's in the realm of Pledges and Promises. It would apply to candidates whether they're sitting judges or not, and that is that a candidate for judicial office shall not pledge or promise certain results in deciding a particular case or issue in a case without regard to the law or facts of the case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-521",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "52180c45b950d77af847337def6a1d14bddbf6876f6a15ff332175c479834fde",
      "evidence_id": "ev-52180c45b950-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Now, if that's what they're trying to do -- do you agree that is what this is trying to do? And, second, if that is what they're trying to do, why is that unconstitutional?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-521",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "52180c45b950d77af847337def6a1d14bddbf6876f6a15ff332175c479834fde",
      "evidence_id": "ev-52180c45b950-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: No, I told you what it means. \"Commit\" means \"commit.\" We can't go more than the words \"commit\" or \"appear to commit,\" other than to illustrate them by example."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "GILBERT",
      "case_id": "2001-01-521",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "52180c45b950d77af847337def6a1d14bddbf6876f6a15ff332175c479834fde",
      "evidence_id": "ev-52180c45b950-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GILBERT: The beginning of the second paragraph, Your Honor. It only restricts judicial candidates from publicly making known how they would decide issues likely to come before them as judges."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-521",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "52180c45b950d77af847337def6a1d14bddbf6876f6a15ff332175c479834fde",
      "evidence_id": "ev-52180c45b950-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, I think you could set up a system where you get advisory opinions, but I don't know that we've ever allowed that to be done in the First Amendment area."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "James Bopp, Jr., on behalf of the petitioners",
      "case_id": "2001-01-521",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "52180c45b950d77af847337def6a1d14bddbf6876f6a15ff332175c479834fde",
      "evidence_id": "ev-52180c45b950-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BOPP: Your Honor, I don't think this is a matter of mathematical precision. The State brief, itself, states two different formulations of the rule. They say, quote, \"It is clear that the clause applies to statements about how they would decide, quote, issues, end of quote, on pages 1 and 47. And then they say it is, quote, \"clear,\" end of quote, that the announced clause applies to statements about cases. And that is on pages 12 and 37. The rule is not even clear in terms of the State's own formulation of its scope. Secondly, as the Joint Appendix indicates on pages 111 through 123, announcing your views also includes simply answering questions on radio interviews or after speeches. It is hardly a remedy for a candidate to call up the board or the office for an oral opinion which is not binding on them about whether or not they can answer a question on the radio."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "James Bopp, Jr., on behalf of the petitioners",
      "case_id": "2001-01-521",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "52180c45b950d77af847337def6a1d14bddbf6876f6a15ff332175c479834fde",
      "evidence_id": "ev-52180c45b950-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And, finally, it is undisputed that the people of Minnesota want an impartial judiciary. Governor Arnie Carlson, at Joint Appendix page 247, said -- who's a State's witness -- that people do not want judges who are pre-committed. Thus, candidates who would make excessive statements, who would appear to be partial, risk defeat at the polls in Minnesota. Thus, the people can be trusted to make the decisions that they, themselves, have conferred upon themselves, as long as they have the information they need to make that choice. The First Amendment guarantees that they should receive that information, which the Announce Clause both prohibits and chills. It is, therefore, unconstitutional."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2001-01-521",
